# Use case delegate pattern

Domain `*UseCase` is a thin entry point. It must not contain business logic in `invoke()` — delegate instead.

## Repository use cases

Delegate directly to a repository method:

```kotlin
class SignInUseCase @Inject constructor(
    signInRepository: SignInRepository,
) : suspend (SignInData) -> AuthTokens by signInRepository::signIn

class ClearAuthTokensUseCase @Inject constructor(
    tokenRepository: TokenRepository,
) : () -> Unit by tokenRepository::clear
```

If mapping belongs in data layer, add a method on the repository (e.g. `getAccessToken()`), then delegate to it.

## Validation use cases

Put rules in an injectable `*Validation` class with `operator fun invoke`, delegate from `Validate*UseCase`:

```kotlin
class EmailValidation @Inject constructor() {
    operator fun invoke(email: String): EmailValidationItem = ...
}

class ValidateEmailUseCase @Inject constructor(
    emailValidation: EmailValidation,
) : (String) -> EmailValidationItem by emailValidation::invoke
```

## Do not

```kotlin
// BAD — logic inside use case
class SignInUseCase @Inject constructor(
    private val repository: SignInRepository,
) {
    suspend operator fun invoke(data: SignInData) = repository.signIn(data)
}
```

Reference: `cft_shift_android` (`SignInUseCase`, `ClearTokenUseCase`, `SaveTokenUseCase`).
